==English==
[[Image:Cockroachcloseup.jpg|thumb|A cockroach.]]

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|es|cucaracha|}}, from {{m|es|cuca||butterfly caterpillars}}, of {{onomatopoeic|en|nocap=1}} origin; see also Greek {{m|grc|κόκκυξ}} and Late Latin {{m|la|cucus}}. Influenced, by means of folk etymology, by {{m|en|cock}} and {{m|en|roach}}. {{doublet|en|cucaracha}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑkɹoʊt͡ʃ/|/ˈkɑkəɹoʊt͡ʃ/|q2=nonstandard|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒkɹəʊt͡ʃ/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cockroach.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q1661828}}Any black or brown [[straight]]-[[winged]] [[insect]] of the order {{taxfmt|Blattodea|order}} that is not a termite.
#: {{syn|en|roach|q1=US}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=121|passage=Then there were '''cockroach''' hunts; we chased them out of their haunts, where at night they busily devoured currants, jam, and sugar; in fact their diet was far more varied than ours, for they made plentiful meals off our boots, the coverings of our books, and any stray pieces of flannel that came in their way.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|offensive}} A person or a member of a group of people regarded as [[undesirable]] and [[rapidly]] [[procreate|procreating]].
# {{lb|en|AU|slang|derogatory|jocular|especially used in|_|rugby league}} A person from [[w:New South Wales|New South Wales]].
# {{lb|en|AU|slang|obsolete}} A hard lump of [[brown sugar]].

